{
  "abstract": "Adding two vertices with arbitrary neighborhoods to any subgraph of either of two nine-chromatic biplanar graphs preserves nine-colorability whenever the enlargement is biplanar. The first graph is a 21-vertex example containing a six-clique joined to a pentagon. Independently replacing both stars at any one vertex by arbitrary plane graphs in their disks also preserves nine-colorability. The proof checks all 1,617,545 pairs of disk fillings and 115,179 boundary partitions for a ten-vertex exterior. A single additional host contact allows every prescribed nine-coloring to extend at any host order. The second graph is G25k on 25 vertices. A reduction for five vertices with at most five exterior neighbors applies the published five-clique identification theorem and the order-twenty coloring theorem. It permits arbitrary changes within the 17-vertex body and between the body and one old exterior vertex. An intact body forces chromatic number exactly nine. An alternative certificate gives 1,188 explicit colorings that reserve separate classes for the two insertions.",
